---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DocCards, Callout } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const CARDS = [
  { icon: '🏗️', title: 'System Design', desc: 'PocketFlow engine, SharedStore state machine, Coder workspace lifecycle, crash recovery, and the design principle behind it all: architecture is the product.', href: '/docs/architecture/system-design' },
  { icon: '👥', title: 'Agent Roles', desc: 'NEXUS, FORGE, SENTINEL, VESSEL, LORE — each agent\'s role, permissions, workspace lifecycle, CLI module, and how they interact through the SharedStore.', href: '/docs/architecture/agent-roles' },
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture' }]}>
  <h1>Architecture</h1>
  <p>
    OpenFlows is built on a simple thesis: <strong>architecture is the product</strong>.
    AI can generate code against a spec, but it can't write the spec. OpenFlows encodes
    the engineering — the architecture, the contracts, the review gates, the recovery
    patterns — so that agents can produce software, not just code.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system runs as two complementary layers. <strong>Coder</strong> governs
    <em>where</em> agents run: ephemeral Terraform-defined workspaces with centrally
    managed LLM keys, SSO identity, and full audit trails. <strong>OpenFlows</strong>
    governs <em>how</em> agents coordinate: a directed flow graph (PocketFlow) where
    five specialized agents communicate through a typed SharedStore, plan before
    executing, review before merging, and self-heal on failure.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="The flow in brief">
    GitHub issue → NEXUS assigns ticket → Coder workspace provisioned → FORGE writes
    PLAN.md → SENTINEL reviews plan → FORGE implements segment by segment → SENTINEL
    evaluates each segment → FORGE opens PR → VESSEL polls CI and squash-merges →
    LORE writes ADR and CHANGELOG → Coder workspace torn down. No human intervention.
  </Callout>

  <DocCards cards={CARDS} />
</DocsLayout>
